comment.body:
My agent flagged this now 🤖 . I hadn't seen this Routes.navigatorKey.currentContext before, so it made more sense to me to use context.mounted instead ... WDYT?

> ####  Use the widget's own `BuildContext`, not `Routes.navigatorKey.currentContext`
> **Where:** `lib/screens/group_member_screen.dart:97-103`
> 
> Today:
> ```dart
> final nav = Routes.navigatorKey.currentContext;
> if (nav == null || !nav.mounted) return;
> Routes.goBack(nav);
> if (!nav.mounted) return;
> StartChatScreen.show(nav, userPubkey: memberPubkey);
> ```
> 
> The widget already has a `BuildContext` in scope — it's the `context` parameter on `build`, which the `useEffect` closure captures synchronously. The post-frame callback can use that directly. Reaching through `Routes.navigatorKey.currentContext` to recover a context the widget already owns:
> 
> - Adds an unnecessary cross-cutting dependency on `Routes.navigatorKey` from a screen file that otherwise has no business knowing about the global navigator key.
> - Forces two `mounted` guards because the resolved context is now untrusted by construction; one `context.mounted` guard on the widget's own context would do.
> - Makes the redirect's correctness depend on the screen still being mounted under the *root* navigator. Today that's always true (`Routes.navigatorKey` IS the GoRouter's navigator — `lib/routes.dart:133`), but if anyone later introduces a `ShellRoute` or `StatefulShellRoute`, the global key stops pointing at the navigator that actually contains `GroupMemberScreen`. The widget-context version is immune to that change because the context resolves whatever navigator the widget is mounted under.
> 
> **Proposed change:**
> ```dart
> WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback((_) {
>   if (!context.mounted) return;
>   Routes.goBack(context);
>   if (!context.mounted) return;
>   StartChatScreen.show(context, userPubkey: memberPubkey);
> });
> ```
> 
> `Routes.goBack` (`lib/routes.dart:514`) and `StartChatScreen.show` both take a `BuildContext` already — this just uses them the way they're meant to be used. Drop the `Routes.navigatorKey` reference from this file entirely; nothing else here needs it.

